   After building this project, you should be able to answer questions like:

   Why did you use an interface for PaymentMethod instead of an abstract class?
   Why is Delivery an abstract class?
   Where did you use runtime polymorphism?
   Which classes have an IS-A relationship?
   Which classes have a HAS-A relationship?
   How would you add a new payment method without changing existing code?
   Which SOLID principles does your design follow?
   If tomorrow the company adds Wallet Payment, which classes need to change?
   How would you extend this console app into a Spring Boot REST API?

   This is much closer to how features are discussed in a real company: you start with business requirements, identify actors and modules, then design classes and decide where interfaces, abstract classes, and polymorphism fit naturally. Once you finish the Core Java version,
   this same design can be reused almost unchanged when you learn Spring Boot.